---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TileCalculator from '../../components/TileCalculator.astro';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Tile Calculator for Kitchen Backsplash | HomeDIYCalcs"
  description="Calculate exactly how many tiles you need for your kitchen backsplash. Free, accurate, and includes standard waste factor."
>
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <div class="mb-12 text-center">
      <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl md:text-5xl text-ink mb-4">
        Tile Calculator for Kitchen Backsplashes
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-ink-muted max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Backsplashes are typically 18 inches tall and run the length of your countertop. Enter your dimensions for an exact tile count.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TileCalculator defaultLength={10} defaultWidth={1.5} defaultTileSize="4x4" />

    <div class="mt-16 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Measuring for a kitchen backsplash</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink space-y-2 mb-8">
        <li>Measure the linear length of your countertop in feet.</li>
        <li>For "Width" enter the height of your backsplash (typically 1.5 ft / 18 inches).</li>
        <li>Subtract any large gaps like windows or appliances.</li>
        <li>Add 15% waste for mosaic or pattern tiles, 10% for standard subway tiles.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Standard backsplash heights</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-muted mb-4">
        Most kitchen backsplashes follow these standards:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink-muted space-y-1 mb-8">
        <li>Standard: 18 inches (1.5 ft) — fills the gap between counter and upper cabinets</li>
        <li>Full height: extends to ceiling — popular for modern kitchens</li>
        <li>Behind stove only: 30 inches tall, width of stove</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-ink mb-2">How many subway tiles for a 10-foot backsplash?</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted">A 10-foot wide, 18-inch tall backsplash is 15 sq ft. With 4x4 tiles (9 per sq ft) and 10% waste, you'll need about 149 tiles.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-ink mb-2">What's the most popular backsplash tile size?</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted">Subway tiles (3x6 or 4x4 inch) are the most common. They're affordable, classic, and easy to install. Larger 12x12 or 12x24 tiles are popular for modern kitchens.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-medium text-ink mb-2">Should I include outlets and switches in my measurement?</h3>
          <p class="text-ink-muted">No — but don't subtract them either. Outlet boxes are small enough that the cut tiles often work out to similar coverage. Just stick with your linear measurements.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>